TI  - Machine learning techniques in sleep apnea detection: A systematic review
JO  - Intelligent Systems with Applications
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.iswa.2026.200693
UR  - https://consensus.app/papers/details/w:0ef2f014-167b-485e-9240-c6b2114e1332/
AB  - Sleep apnea is a common sleep-related disorder with important health consequences, and machine learning offers a promising way to improve early and scalable diagnosis through physiological signal analysis. This systematic review examined machine learning techniques for sleep apnea detection, with emphasis on model performance, feature extraction, application trends, and current research gaps. The review used a structured approach involving literature search, study screening based on predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria, data extraction on machine learning methods and quality measures, and assessment of the included studies. The findings show that CNNs are effective for spatial features in polysomnography and spectrogram data, recurrent models such as RNNs, LSTMs, and GRUs are better for temporal patterns in respiratory and oxygen saturation signals, and hybrid models often provide stronger overall performance. Multimodal feature extraction improves robustness and accuracy, while lightweight models are more suitable for mobile and low-resource settings. However, the literature is still limited by poor generalizability, overfitting, limited data diversity, inconsistent evaluation criteria, and challenges in clinical translation. Future research should focus on more efficient algorithms, improved feature extraction beyond ECG and RR intervals, better signal quality and dataset diversity, stronger external validation, fairness across populations, and reliable real-time systems for practical clinical use, especially for mild sleep apnea detection.
